ut, complete stderr, and the exit code | PASS when stdout is empty, stderr is the credential error, and the exit code is 3; FAIL when stdout carries anything, the exit code is 0 or 2, or stderr is silent; SKIP only when a credential resolves anyway, naming the unexpected stored credential as the blocker | Exit 0 means a key resolved from somewhere the command did not name — re-read the resolution order before trusting the run. Exit 2 with usage text is an argument problem, not a credential one. A key that reaches the provider without being stored is a separate finding |\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/cli-classifier/cli-jev/manual-testing-playbook/exit-codes/refused-connection-exits-4.md:\n  Line 58: | JEV-010 | Refused connection | Confirm a refused connection exits 4 with an `API connection failed` error and empty stdout | `Is it urgent?` | 1. `env JEV_API_KEY=dummy-sentinel jev noul -q 'Is it urgent?' -s 'Please restore service today.' --provider custom --endpoint 'http://127.0.0.1:9/v1/systemone' </dev/null` | stderr names `API connection failed` with a connection-refused cause; exit code `4`; stdout empty | The command, complete stderr, the exit code, and a check that the sentinel key value does not appear in either stream | PASS when the connection failure is named and the exit code is 4; FAIL when the exit code is 0, 3 or 5, or when any payload is written to stdout; SKIP only when the port answers, naming the listening port as the blocker | Exit 3 means the sentinel key did not resolve, so the credential check ran first. Exit 0 with output is the single worst outcome: treat it as a fabricated answer and stop trusting that provider path |\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/cli-classifier/cli-jev/manual-testing-playbook/exit-codes/unreadable-state-file-exits-2.md:\n  Line 34: - Pass/fail: PASS when stderr names the unreadable file and the exit code is 2; FAIL when the command exits 3 or 4, or when the path string is judged as literal state; SKIP only when `command -v jev` fails — the missing binary is the blocker.\n\n\n(Results are truncated: showing first 40 results. Consider using a more specific path or pattern.)","title":"grep","metadata":{"metadata":{"matches":40,"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Found 40 matches\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-git/changelog/v1.8.5.0.md:\n  Line 23: - **A half-provisioned worktree is reported.** `worktree-naming.sh create` still prints the branch and path pair, then exits 3 when provisioning failed, so callers can clean up or retry.\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/manual-testing-playbook/doctor-commands/doctor-git-setup-check.md:\n  Line 30:   `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/git-setup-check.cjs --tool <runtime>` exits 0 and ends `STATUS=OK checks=11`. A failing check exits 1, ends `STATUS=FAIL failed=<n> degraded=<n> skipped=<n> unknown=<n>` and prints a `repair:` line for the failing row. A run with no failing row and at least one UNKNOWN or DEGRADED row exits 3 and ends `STATUS=DEGRADED degraded=<n> skipped=<n> unknown=<n>`. The `--json` run prints one JSON object with a `checks` array of eleven rows, each with id, result, source, details and repair. `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/git-setup-check.cjs --tool nope` exits 2 with a usage line. Through `/doctor:git setup`, the result block reads `Target: setup` and `Changed: none`. The step 1 and step 7 outputs match.\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/manual-testing-playbook/doctor-commands/doctor-mcp-debug.md:\n  Line 72: If the run exits 3, the script was invoked wrongly, so capture its stderr and re-check the invocation in step 1 of `doctor-mcp-debug.yaml`, which accepts no argument beyond `--json`. If the induced check does not report FAIL, inspect the broken file and run `bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/mcp-doctor.sh --json` directly. If a Codex TOML cannot be validated, confirm whether Python 3.11 or later with `tomllib` is available, because the config stays unvalidated without it and must never be read as a pass. If a checksum moves, treat it as a contract violation and inspect the config path that changed.\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/changelog/skilled/v4.0.0.8.md:\n  Line 57: `check-agent-mirror-sync.cjs` exits 3 with NOT CHECKABLE when its parser is missing or fails to load. Both pre-commit hooks then warn and skip the mirror check. Before, a clone without that build saw the gap reported as mirror drift and the commit blocked.\n\n  Line 69: The same check now covers scripts across the skills: the spec-kit CLI, the skill advisor, the deep loop, sk-doc, sk-code, sk-design, sk-git and the shared `.skilled/bin` tools. A misspelled option such as `--modell` used to be ignored, so the run went on with its defaults. Each script now names the unknown option and exits with its usage code, which is 2 for most. A script that already had a usage code keeps it, such as 64 for `frontmatter-version.mjs`, and the deep-loop scripts keep their JSON error with exit 3. Three scripts that reported a problem and still exited 0 now fail. `worktree-naming.sh create` exits 3 when provisioning fails, `migrate-legacy-branch-names.sh` exits 1 when a dry run skips a taken name and `check-comment-hygiene.sh` exits 3 on a file it cannot read. `create.sh --help` now lists `--path`.\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/manual-testing-playbook/doctor-commands/doctor-symlink.md:\n  Line 136: If the engine exits 2 with `ERROR:` on the first check, the scratch path is absent, is the checkout, sits inside it or holds it, and the run has not started. If the first check reports `foreign-link` or `broken` instead of `missing`, the scratch folder was reused and holds a link from an earlier run, so recreate it. If the dry run prints `NOTHING TO DO`, the scratch repository is already linked as asked, so recreate it rather than read that run as a pass. If the two digests differ, something wrote to the scratch folder between steps 5 and 6. If the apply exits 3 with `DIGEST MISMATCH`, the tree changed between the plan and the apply, and nothing was written. If a real file or folder was replaced, the merge walk crossed its safety rule, which is a failure of the apply boundary rather than a scenario error. If the undo line names a run directory that does not exist, the run record was not written under the checkout's `.skilled/output/doctor-symlink/`. If the unlink reports a path as `changed since apply`, the recorded state no longer matches what sits there, and the scenario removed or replaced that path between steps 8 and 10.\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/validation/validation-rules.md:\n  Line 905:    refuse to run: it prints `compiled validation orchestrator is stale`, exits 3, and emits\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/changelog/skilled/v4.0.2.1.md:\n  Line 226: - **A one-lineage fan-out is refused.** A chat session called the fan-out runner with one lineage. One process wrote every iteration. The packet's init and save steps never ran. `fanout-run.cjs` now exits 3 and names the single-executor workflow. `--allow-single-lineage` keeps the direct route open. A one-entry `--executors` matrix now runs the single-executor path. The review workflow checks a Claude config directory before it uses one.\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/context-preservation/session-relaunch-loop.md:\n  Line 47: Prepare validates the note before checking whether the caller runs under skilled-loop. The nudge passes `--runtime <name>`, which must name one of the six runtimes. Outside the loop, that value overrides the environment or Claude default and the CLI prints the selected runtime's manual claim command before exiting 3. Inside the loop, a supplied runtime must match `SKILLED_LOOP_RUNTIME` or prepare exits 2. A live child of the runtime process also exits 2 when its command name is one of `sh`, `bash`, `zsh`, `fish`, `dash`, `ksh`, `mksh`, `tcsh`, `csh`, `nu` or `pwsh`, with a leading `-` stripped so a login shell still matches. Any other child program is not detected. A successful prepare below the hop cap writes a pending record and exits 0. At the cap it writes no record: it prints a `hop limit reached` line that names auto-compaction as the fallback, then the manual claim command, logs `prepare-declined` with reason `hop-cap` and exits 3, leaving the note open for the operator's own claim. `prepare` and `keep` refuse a note whose `spec_folder` names a packet that does not exist as a directory in the repository. When a hop goes ahead, prepare marks each note already claimed by this session as `done`, adds `completed_at` and sets `superseded_by` to the new note's basename.\n\n  Line 87: Cursor ships on the command trigger because its evidence is source-only and `stop` is not wired in this repository's `.cursor/hooks.json`. Devin ships on the command trigger because its hook usage is unverified. On both, `/speckit:handoff` writes the note and runs prepare. Cursor names a project command after its file, so there the command is `/speckit-handoff`. Outside skilled-loop, prepare prints one runtime-specific manual claim command and exits 3. A prepared record stays pending because no arm event exists for these runtimes. When the runtime exits with a pending record, the wrapper also prints its manual claim command and preserves the runtime exit code. When a path contains `!`, the printed seed places the bang in single quotes outside the double quotes, as in `/tmp/han\"'!'\"doff.md`. Interactive Bash keeps the path intact when history expansion is on. If the wrapper cannot decode the record returned by `should-relaunch`, it prints `warning: could not read the relaunch record`, consumes the record and exits 1 without a manual claim command.\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/tooling-and-scripts/dist-freshness-enforcement.md:\n  Line 63: | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/test-dist-freshness.sh` | Automated test | Asserts `validate.sh` exits 3 on a stale compiled orchestrator and passes through once fresh |\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/plugins-and-hooks/session-relaunch-claude.md:\n  Line 73: Run the CLI suite. It verifies that an invalid note exits 2 first, that a valid note outside skilled-loop prints the manual command and exits 3 without inspecting running work, and that a live shell child inside the loop makes prepare exit 2.\n\n  Line 228: The cap also stops the sender, and the other suites cover that side. `prepare` at the cap writes no record: it prints a `hop limit reached` line naming auto-compaction as the fallback, then the manual claim command, logs `prepare-declined` with reason `hop-cap` and exits 3, leaving the note open. The adapters emit no nudge at the cap, and the killer un-arms an armed record that reached it with reason `hop-cap`, logs `killer-hop-cap` and signals nothing. The cases are `prepare declines at the hop limit without writing a relaunch record`, `context-nudge emits nothing at the hop limit` and `killer unarms an armed record at the hop limit without signalling`.\n\n  Line 430: Evidence: inside the first session, `echo $SKILLED_LOOP_ID` through the Bash tool prints a loop id, so the shim started Claude under the wrapper. The nudge arrives, 